**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 30 documents across UNICEF, WHO, WFP, World Bank, ENA, UNESCO, UNHCR, and World Bank archive pages, then kept 11 new source-claim units plus 3 reused items.
- Duplicates removed: the UNICEF Ethiopia annual report appeared in several search hits; the May 16 Ministry of Finance agreement duplicated the April 12 World Bank conflict-affected-communities project; the broader World Bank and WFP country pages repeated claims already captured in the targeted 2022 articles.
- Blocked or inaccessible items removed: UNHCR node and situation pages returned 402/404, and some ecoi mirrors exposed only index shells without the underlying report text.
- Out-of-period or low-value items removed: 2024-2026 digital ID, PSNP-6, and education-video pages were either too far outside 2022 or too generic to improve ruler-specific attribution.
- I also treated some sources as context only when they were useful for baseline or constraint setting but did not add clean ruler attribution, especially sanitation, drought, and retrospective education trend sources.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on Abiy Ahmed's own welfare priorities, as distinct from donor-supported programs and inherited safety nets, remains thin.
- Better 2022 household outcome data would help, especially malnutrition, learning loss, sanitation access, and region-by-region coverage for vulnerable groups.
- More direct execution evidence would help for budget outturns, service quality, and whether the 2022 priorities in the budget and financing agreements were actually implemented on time and at scale.

